<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-outcomes-week-08-09">
  <title>Week 8 and Week 9</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      These weeks focused on the natural exponential function and its applications.
      We computed derivatives involving <m>e^u</m> using the product, quotient, and chain rules,
      built exponential and power law models through two given points,
      and rewrote general powers using the natural exponential and natural logarithm.
      We then used that idea to develop logarithmic differentiation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E2">
    <title>E2 Derivatives with the Natural Exponential</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E2">
      <title>E2</title>
      <p>
        I can compute derivatives using products, quotients and compositions involving <m>e^u</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E2_Derivatives_Natural_Exponential">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f(x) = x^2 e^{-2x}</m>, find <m>f'(x)</m>, complete sign analysis on <m>f'(x)</m>,
          and then interpret the positive and negative intervals to describe the shape of <m>y=f(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E3">
    <title>E3 Exponential and Power Law Models</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E3">
      <title>E3</title>
      <p>
        I can solve for variables in the exponent to create exponential and power law models
        passing through two given points.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E3_Exponential_Power_Law_Models">
      <statement>
        <p>
          An exponential model depending on time <m>t</m> can be represented by <m>y = Ae^{r(t-t_0)}</m>.
          A population of bacteria is measured to have 200 cells after <m>t=20</m> minutes.
          It has 600 cells after <m>t=60</m> minutes.
          Find the exponential model for the population.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <remark>
      <p>
        A checkpoint model could also use a power law model, which would use a formula
        <m>y=A(x/x_0)^p</m>. You will be provided the basic model equation.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E4">
    <title>E4 Rewriting Powers</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E4">
      <title>E4</title>
      <p>
        I can rewrite any power involving a positive base by using the natural exponential
        with base <m>e</m> and natural logarithm.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E4_Rewriting_Powers">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite <m>\pi^{\sqrt{2}\, t}</m> as <m>e^{kt}</m> for some number <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E5">
    <title>E5 Logarithmic Differentiation</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E5">
      <title>E5</title>
      <p>
        I can use logarithmic differentiation on <m>y=f(x)</m> by differentiating <m>\ln(|y|)</m>
        with the chain rule to solve for <m>y' = f'(x)</m> after taking advantage of the expanded logarithm.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E5_Logarithmic_Differentiation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use logarithmic differentiation to find <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left[ x^{\ln(x)} \right]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
